Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> On 06/09/10 18:02, Patric Mueller wrote:
>> 
>> The scroll of amnesia has been replaced with the scroll of flood.
>
> Is this an instant-drown function, or similar to spilling fountain water
> (i.e. effecting random squares around the player)?

A scroll of flood will never generate a pool under the player if all
squares around the player are not safe.

If there are safe squares uncursed may and cursed will create a pool
under the player and if that happens the player may drown because of
normal game mechanics (exploding acid potions, too burdened because of
loadstone, etc.).

I didn't feel like adding another instadeath possibility just because
the player might be trying out an unknown item.

>> [...]
>> All these features come with a cost so save and bones files from 3.5.2
>> and earlier aren't compatible with 3.5.3.
>
> (I thought it would be common practise to increase a higher-level release
> number for incompatibility changes. Hmm. Anyway.)

If I did that I'd be at 3.8.0. :)

I don't have to care for bones and save game compatibility for the
public server as old binaries are kept. It is really nice if you don't
have to worry about that.

But you can look at the version number in the nowadays more standard
way. Even numbered versions are stable releases and odd numbered
versions are development releases.

So 3.5.x is still the development version where anything goes and
3.6.0 will be the first stable version. If the next version will be
3.5.4 or 3.6.0 depends on if I get everything that I think really
needs to be fixed into the next version or not.

>> User interface improvements
>> 
>>   * automatically talk to monsters if only one is next to the player
>
> In vanilla I always wondered why there's an option to address individual
> shopkeepers in line-of-sight when #chat'ing. Probably to cuss at the
> other shopkeeper? Or to shout; "See, here I get much better prices than
> in your shop!"? :-}

You can talk to empty space and if there is an invisible monster that
you don't see it will reply nevertheless.

But this is an extreme edge case and I didn't think that it would be
harmful to exclude this possibility.

>>   * Ctrl-e automatically writes resp. engraves "Elbereth"
>
> How is that implemented in case you have a couple choices, like a hard
> gem, a cursed and an uncursed athame, and a wand of fire or lightning.
> Is there a user dialog to select one option, or some preselected tool
> (similar to Q/quiver), or some "intelligence" implemented?

Only some really small intelligence. If you are wielding an athame it
will use that. Otherwise it will use your fingers.

I considered enabling switching to an uncursed athame but the wielding
code was too convoluted so I didn't implement that.

>>   * Pi day support
>
> What's that, may I ask?

To commemorate pi day, cream pies are renamed on March 15th to
irrational pies. Not as useful as towel day, I suppose. :)

>>   * Magic lamps may be polymorphed or wished for
>
> Hmm.. - while I think the latter is nice, I am uncertain about the
> polymorph feature, given that there are so many guaranteed unihorns.
> IIRC, you can even revive unicorns with yet another horn so that you
> can get many unihorns from a single unicorn by killing and reviving.

As magic lamps in UnNetHack don't give full wishes anymore, there was
just no need anymore to restrict the wishing or polymorphing of them.

